{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["And the reason for <b>\"Thus says YHWH, the Elohim of Israel\"</b> is that the worshippers of the calf intended to sacrifice to the Elohim of Israel, yet behold, the attribute of Justice struck them because they cut off the shoots. Furthermore, <b>\"for the judgment is Elohim's\"</b> (Deuteronomy 1:17). And because of this it is said of the Levites: <b>\"For the Elohim of Israel has separated you from the congregation of Israel\"</b> (Numbers 16:9), for the service belongs to the Elohim of Israel, and for His name they were distinguished through this merit." "<b>\"Put every man his sword upon his thigh\"</b> — the worshippers of the calf were numerous, and it was not possible to bring them before a court of three, therefore he commanded all the sons of Levi to gird their swords, in the manner of what our Rabbis said (Sanhedrin 45b) that if you cannot put him to death by the death prescribed for him, you may put him to death by any death you are able. Now this was a provisional ruling for the hour, to sanctify the Name, for there was no warning among them — for who warned them? But the sons of Levi recognized in those who were killed that they had been its worshippers. And according to the opinion of the one who says (Yoma 66b) that witnesses and warning were in the possession of man, as Rashi wrote (Rashi on Exodus 32:20), we say that he commanded them to gird their swords in order to seize them and bring them before Moses or the Sanhedrin, and whomever they found with witnesses and warning, they killed by stoning according to the law of idolaters, or perhaps their death was by the sword like a seduced city. And all the sons of Levi belonged to HaShem, and they were warning the people not to bow down to it and not to sacrifice to it, for Aaron did not command to feast except to HaShem alone, as I have explained. And the correct view in my eyes is according to the one who says (Yoma ibid.): one who sacrificed and offered incense — by the sword; one who embraced and kissed — by death; one who rejoiced in his heart — by striding, as is found in tractate Yoma. Now all of this was a provisional act for the hour, for one is not strangled and kissed by death for all generations (Sanhedrin 60b)." "And the reason for <b>\"and kill every man his brother\"</b> is that you shall not spare and shall not cover for brother, friend, or relative. And the reason for <b>\"Thus says YHWH, the Elohim of Israel\"</b> — it is not part of the commandment of <b>\"He who sacrifices to elohim shall be destroyed\"</b> (Exodus 22:19), for these were not liable to death by law, but rather it is a commandment that was said to Moses from the mouth of the Gevurah and was not written down, for when the honored HaShem relented from the evil, He commanded him: since you do not want Me to consume them, you shall kill their worshippers by the sword, in the manner of <b>\"Take all the chiefs of the people and hang them up to HaShem before the sun, and the anger of YHWH shall turn away from Israel\"</b> (Numbers 25:4). Now this commandment is like the commandment of <b>\"This is the thing which YHWH has commanded: Let an omerful of it be kept\"</b> (Exodus 16:32). And I have already mentioned (above, 10:2, 11a) similar cases."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {1 "The phrase 'בְּהִדְרוֹקָן' is translated hyper-literally as 'by striding,' following the Talmudic context (Yoma 66b) which discusses רוקד — a form of skipping/dancing before the calf — as one category of worship. The text is elliptical; the meaning is that each act of worship received a corresponding mode of execution."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:20:33.565024125Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}